\pard\keepn\sb360\sa180\f0\b\fs34\cf2 \u-27068?\u24405?\u-164?Technical Walkthrough \u-32015?\u25991?\u32451?\u20064?\b0\par
\pard\keepn\sb260\sa100\f0\b\fs26\cf3 1. What does the application do?\b0\par
\pard\li260\ri180\sa180\sl310\slmult1\f1\i\fs22\cf4\highlight6 It gathers public Xero pages, stores the extracted evidence, retrieves relevant passages, and uses Gemini to answer questions with validated citations.\highlight0\i0\par
\pard\keepn\sb260\sa100\f0\b\fs26\cf3 2. How is the system structured?\b0\par
\pard\li260\ri180\sa180\sl310\slmult1\f1\i\fs22\cf4\highlight6 The React frontend handles user interaction. The Express backend handles research, retrieval, model calls, validation, and SQLite storage.\highlight0\i0\par
\pard\keepn\sb260\sa100\f0\b\fs26\cf3 3. Why did you use TypeScript?\b0\par
\pard\li260\ri180\sa180\sl310\slmult1\f1\i\fs22\cf4\highlight6 TypeScript helps detect mismatched data shapes before runtime, especially between the frontend, backend, database, and model response.\highlight0\i0\par
\pard\keepn\sb260\sa100\f0\b\fs26\cf3 4. Why did you choose SQLite?\b0\par
\pard\li260\ri180\sa180\sl310\slmult1\f1\i\fs22\cf4\highlight6 The dataset is small and local, so SQLite provides durable storage without requiring a separate database server.\highlight0\i0\par
\pard\keepn\sb260\sa100\f0\b\fs26\cf3 5. How does retrieval work?\b0\par
\pard\li260\ri180\sa180\sl310\slmult1\f1\i\fs22\cf4\highlight6 The application scores stored chunks using BM25 keyword relevance, returns the top five, and labels them E1 to E5 for the current question.\highlight0\i0\par
\pard\keepn\sb260\sa100\f0\b\fs26\cf3 6. How do you prevent unsupported answers?\b0\par
\pard\li260\ri180\sa180\sl310\slmult1\f1\i\fs22\cf4\highlight6 Weak retrieval skips the model. For strong retrieval, the backend rejects unknown or inconsistent citation IDs.\highlight0\i0\par
\pard\keepn\sb260\sa100\f0\b\fs26\cf3 7. How is research reused?\b0\par
\pard\li260\ri180\sa180\sl310\slmult1\f1\i\fs22\cf4\highlight6 A normal gather reuses complete stored sources without fetching them again. Refresh explicitly fetches and processes the sources again.\highlight0\i0\par
\pard\keepn\sb260\sa100\f0\b\fs26\cf3 8. How do you handle external failures?\b0\par
\pard\li260\ri180\sa180\sl310\slmult1\f1\i\fs22\cf4\highlight6 The application reports the failure and does not present an unsupported answer. Existing research keeps its original retrieval time.\highlight0\i0\par
\pard\keepn\sb260\sa100\f0\b\fs26\cf3 9. How did you test it?\b0\par
\pard\li260\ri180\sa180\sl310\slmult1\f1\i\fs22\cf4\highlight6 I added seventeen credential-free Vitest tests covering extraction, persistence, retrieval, citation validation, and safe failures.\highlight0\i0\par
\pard\keepn\sb260\sa100\f0\b\fs26\cf3 10. What is the main limitation?\b0\par
\pard\li260\ri180\sa180\sl310\slmult1\f1\i\fs22\cf4\highlight6 Retrieval is keyword-based, and citation validation checks IDs and formatting but cannot fully prove semantic support.\highlight0\i0\par
\pard\keepn\sb260\sa100\f0\b\fs26\cf3 11. What would you change if the dataset grew?\b0\par
\pard\li260\ri180\sa180\sl310\slmult1\f1\i\fs22\cf4\highlight6 I would keep the API contract and replace the retrieval implementation with SQLite full-text search or hybrid vector retrieval, then evaluate it against the same questions.\highlight0\i0\par
\pard\keepn\sb260\sa100\f0\b\fs26\cf3 12. How did you review AI-assisted code?\b0\par
\pard\li260\ri180\sa180\sl310\slmult1\f1\i\fs22\cf4\highlight6 I reviewed a citation-validation edge case where grouped citations were rejected, corrected the parser, and added a regression test for E1 and E2 together.\highlight0\i0\par
